{
 "id": "2026-11-02-white-dwarf-double-rings",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {"id": "hook", "text": "A dead star has two rings of gas around it. What carved them?"},
  {"id": "f1", "text": "Astronomers used the Very Large Telescope in Chile to study a white dwarf, the dead core of a sun-like star, about sixteen hundred light years away. Its light revealed not one disc of gas, but two rings with a gap between them."},
  {"id": "f2", "text": "The study, published in Nature Astronomy, says the rings differ in chemistry. Magnesium shows up only in the inner ring. The team's best explanation is two surviving rocky bodies that release gas where they meet dust. Nobody has seen them directly."},
  {"id": "f3", "text": "There is a caveat. The authors also consider that the star may be two white dwarfs, too close to tell apart. Either way, they say it challenges models of how such discs form, and may hint at our own Solar System's future."},
  {"id": "cta", "text": "Will astronomers find those hidden bodies by twenty thirty? Yes or no?"}
 ],
